Summary: Rosetta 2: refuse a program's own LDT entries, as Wine 10 did, instead of hanging
Category: cpu
Kind: workaround
Origin: wine-mac-builds
Upstream: candidate (draft in .local/bug-reports/, with
 krnl386-attach-without-16bit)
Why: Wine 11 implements NtSetLdtEntries for wow64 processes and passes the
 entry to i386_set_ldt. Rosetta 2 can't serve 16-bit segments: for any
 16-bit entry it prints "rosetta error: LDT ... not supported" and the
 thread never returns (a native test process aborts). 32-bit entries are
 accepted, but only flat ones can be loaded into a data segment register.
 Wine 10's NtSetLdtEntries returned STATUS_NOT_IMPLEMENTED on 64-bit macOS
 and programs carried on; under Rosetta, return that again. Wine's own
 32-bit code and %fs selectors don't go through NtSetLdtEntries.
 Seen in Bioscopia (Director 8): it stops on its first title frame after
 "rosetta error: LDT 0xf00f3210488323f not supported" (base 0x0f210488,
 limit 0x323f, 16-bit data, DPL 3). A 32-bit test program setting that
 descriptor spins at 100% CPU on macfixes 11.22-build3; with this patch
 the call returns 0xc0000002 and the program continues.
Programs: Bioscopia: stops on its first title frame after "rosetta error:
 LDT ... not supported" (Wine 11)
Seen on: macOS 26.7.2 (M1 Max): i386_set_ldt with any 16-bit entry aborts a
 native process, and a 32-bit data entry with a non-zero base aborts it
 when loaded into %es (native probe)
Uncertain: measured on macOS 26.7.2 only. A program whose LDT entry is a
 flat 32-bit data segment would work under Rosetta and is now refused, as
 on Wine 10.
Expects before it: ntdll-is-apple-silicon; is_apple_silicon()
Status: 11.22: 2026-11-16: built in wine-macfixes-11.22-build4;
 ldt-program-selector hangs on build3 and passes on build4. Bioscopia
 passes on build4 (Second Chance integration run on meeka): no LDT error,
 picture centred. 2026-11-25: the quick build returns 0xc0000002.
 11.21: 2026-11-16: built in wine-macfixes-11.21-build5;
 ldt-program-selector passes there. Not yet tried with Bioscopia.

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
index dfa8f2f..b608594 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
@@ -4153,6 +4153,10 @@ NTSTATUS ldt_get_entry( WORD sel, CLIENT_ID client_id, LDT_ENTRY *entry )
     return status;
 }
 
+#ifdef __APPLE__
+static int is_apple_silicon(void);
+#endif
+
 /******************************************************************************
  *           NtSetLdtEntries   (NTDLL.@)
  *           ZwSetLdtEntries   (NTDLL.@)
@@ -4163,6 +4167,13 @@ NTSTATUS WINAPI NtSetLdtEntries( ULONG sel1, ULONG entry1_low, ULONG entry1_high
     union { LDT_ENTRY entry; ULONG ul[2]; } entry;
 
     if (is_win64 && !is_wow64()) return STATUS_NOT_IMPLEMENTED;
+#ifdef __APPLE__
+    /* Rosetta 2 serves only the LDT entries Wine sets up itself (32-bit code
+     * and %fs). It stops the whole process on others, such as a program's
+     * 16-bit data selector ("rosetta error: LDT ... not supported"). Refuse
+     * them, as Wine 10 did on 64-bit macOS. */
+    if (is_apple_silicon()) return STATUS_NOT_IMPLEMENTED;
+#endif
     if (sel1 >> 16 || sel2 >> 16) return STATUS_INVALID_LDT_DESCRIPTOR;
 
     server_enter_uninterrupted_section( &virtual_mutex, &sigset );
